This study introduces a Cartesian decomposition method for embedding a group $G$ into a wreath product $H \wr K$. This technique offers a streamlined proof of the embedding of the symmetric group $S_n$ into the affine group $AGL(d, q)$, where $n = q^d$. Furthermore, it provides a concise demonstration of the embedding of $S_n$ into the automorphism group of the Hamming graph $H(d, q)$, with $n = q^d$. Finally, we establish that the embedding of $S_n$ into $AGL(d, q)$ is optimal when $n = q^d$. These results are significant for applications in cryptography and coding theory. The proposed Cartesian decomposition approach is also applicable to other embedding problems, such as embedding $S_n$ into $S_m$ where $n \leq m$. This work extends the findings of the first author, who previously addressed the embedding of $S_n$ into $AGL(d, q)$ for $n = q^d$ in 2017. (This abstract was written by the authors.)
